 "performances": [
  {"date": "1987-08-09", "venue": "Nectar's", "city": "Burlington", "state": "VT", "gap": null, "set": "2", "jamchart": true, "jam": "The debut includes both the highly composed section we now think of as The Curtain, as well as the flowing and spriralling instrumental section familiar to us as the With jam. Melodies and musical ideas from this classic would later be incorporated into \"Reba\" and \"Rift,\" while songs which debuted subsequently, especially \"Mockingbird\" and \"Esther,\" both seem to draw inspiration from With.", "note": "First known performance.", "next": "Halley's Comet", "xprev": "Funky Bitch", "nb": 1},
  {"date": "1987-08-10", "venue": "Nectar's", "city": "Burlington", "state": "VT", "gap": 0, "set": "2", "prev": "Fee", "next": "I Know a Little", "nb": 1},
  {"date": "1987-08-21", "venue": "Ian McLean's Farm", "city": "Hebron", "state": "NY", "gap": 0, "set": "1", "jamchart": true, "jam": "Gotta love the dogs, Marley and/or others barking in the background throughout. The Curtain section is very precise, and filled with a sense of energy. Listening along, you can imagine the band had practiced the composed section extensively. The With jam begins with delicate, lofty soloing by Trey, then gradually builds up to a crescendo, before easing back down and fading into the ether.", "prev": "Clod", "next": "Light Up Or Leave Me Alone", "nb": 1},
  {"date": "1987-08-29", "venue": "The Ranch", "city": "South Burlington", "state": "VT", "gap": 1, "set": "2", "jamchart": true, "jam": "Fish and Mike are noticeably strong in The Curtain section, despite some minor precision issues from Trey and Page. After the anthemic repeat which would evolve into \"Rift,\" this With jam really takes off into some stunning and inspired playing by Trey, strengthened by colorful and delicate Page counterpoint. Trey's spirited melodies from about 10:25 - 11:00 sound remarkably similar to the as yet unhatched jam in \"Mockingbird.\" The cathartic settle seems the perfect ending.", "prev": "Swing Low, Sweet Chariot", "next": "McGrupp and the Watchful Hosemasters", "nb": 1},
  {"date": "1987-09-21", "venue": "Nectar's", "city": "Burlington", "state": "VT", "gap": 5, "set": "2", "prev": "You Enjoy Myself", "next": "Big Black Furry Creature from Mars", "nb": 1},
  {"date": "1988-02-07", "venue": "Nectar's", "city": "Burlington", "state": "VT", "gap": 18, "set": "3", "jamchart": true, "jam": "This impressive version begins with a nearly flawless Curtain section, led by intese Fish action. In With, Trey's tone is measured and confident, bolstered by strong support from Mike and Fish. After reaching a peak, the jam takes on a more rocking, almost \"DEG\"-like vibe, beginning at about 12:55, sounding very different from the typical With jam, before ending with the traditional diminuendo conclusion.", "prev": "Bundle of Joy", "next": "The Ballad of Curtis Loew", "nb": 1},
  {"date": "1988-05-15", "venue": "Beecher Hill Farm", "city": "Hinesburg", "state": "VT", "gap": 28, "set": "2", "jamchart": true, "jam": "Although The Curtain seems to hit a few minor bumps, these are offset by a strong, lift-off type jam in With. Beginning with a light, weightless sense, the jam becomes more grounded and intense as it develops, with Trey's spiralling soloing reaching an intial peak. \"Fly Famous Mockingbird\" debuted in February, 1988, and if you listen closely from about 12:40 - 13:30, you'll notice striking similarities between these instrumental sections.", "out": ">", "prev": "McGrupp and the Watchful Hosemasters", "next": "Wilson", "nb": 1},
  {"date": "1988-05-22", "venue": "Nectar's", "city": "Burlington", "state": "VT", "gap": 0, "set": "3", "prev": "Take the 'A' Train", "next": "Suzy Greenberg", "nb": 1},
  {"date": "1988-05-24", "venue": "Nectar's", "city": "Burlington", "state": "VT", "gap": 1, "set": "1", "jamchart": true, "jam": "A well-executed Curtain - not spotless, but few, if any, are. This With jam is evocative and captures a sense of weigtlessness, soaring above the musical clouds. Many of these early With jams move beyond this uplifting section, exploring more dissonant, rocking terrain. Not so here, as the band seems content to let this one gracefully recede, following a soaring peak.", "next": "Rocky Top", "first": 1, "nb": 1},
  {"date": "1988-06-19", "venue": "Nectar's", "city": "Burlington", "state": "VT", "gap": 5, "set": "1", "jamchart": true, "jam": "On this recording, Page is more prominent in the mix, which provides a nice audio record of his important role with this song. While The Curtain is generally tight, minor bumps here and there point to the difficulty of performing this highly composed section. The With jam begins with some nice, quiet interplay between Trey and Page. The jam gradually builds energy, but remains largely undulating and uplifting straight through to the downshifting conclusion.", "next": "Funky Bitch", "first": 1, "nb": 1},
  {"date": "1988-07-11", "venue": "Sam's Tavern", "city": "Burlington", "state": "VT", "gap": 8, "set": "1", "prev": "Suzy Greenberg", "next": "Funky Bitch", "nb": 1},
  {"date": "1988-07-23", "venue": "Pete's Phabulous Phish Phest", "city": "Underhill", "state": "VT", "gap": 1, "set": "2", "times": 2, "jamchart": true, "jam": "An unprecedented and highly improvisational version, which includes an unknown additional percussionist. Following a strong run through The Curtain section, the band breaks into the familiar With jam. However, this jam quickly takes on a more dissonant and frenetic character, with \"DEG\"-like undertones, although it retains some semblance of With through about the 15:00 mark. After this point, the band breaks for \"DEG,\" followed by some abstract and crazed jamming, a testament to the band's nascent improvisational nature. Eventually, the playing returns to the customary With ending.", "note": "Unknown additional percussionist.", "out": "->", "next": "Dave's Energy Guide", "xprev": "Fire", "nb": 1},
  {"date": "1988-07-29", "venue": "The Roma", "city": "Telluride", "state": "CO", "gap": 3, "set": "1", "prev": "Sneakin' Sally Thru the Alley", "next": "Fire", "nb": 1},
  {"date": "2000-07-12", "venue": "Deer Creek Music Center", "city": "Noblesville", "state": "IN", "gap": 1179, "set": "1", "prev": "My Friend, My Friend", "next": "Tube", "nb": 1},
  {"date": "2000-09-17", "venue": "Merriweather Post Pavilion", "city": "Columbia", "state": "MD", "gap": 9, "set": "1", "jamchart": true, "jam": "Compared to the well-known bustout version from Deer Creek in 2000, this version features much better execution in The Curtain and a more competent delivery in the With jam. Trey's use of the Leslie during the early, \"Rift\" part of With is a cool plus, and the band coalesces nicely from there, for a snappy and spirited instrumental jam.", "out": ">", "prev": "Fluffhead", "next": "Chalk Dust Torture", "nb": 1},
